## What is clean data?

Clean (or Tidy) data is formatted in a structured way, ready for analysis. It is ideally free from errors, missing values and other general inconsistencies that would impede analysis.

-   no duplicate rows/values
-   free from spelling errors
-   contains the correct data types (e.g. numeric data is not stored as a character)
-   special characters are removed unless specifically needed
-   Nulls

## Tidy Data principles

Tidy data was originally coined by Hadley Wickam, and follows a series of guiding principles. [Ref](https://vita.had.co.nz/papers/tidy-data.pdf)

The data must be structured such that: - each row is an observation - each column is a variable measured - data can be tabulated easily

### Messy data

```{r tidy example, echo=FALSE}
#Insert images here

messy <- tibble(
  `Patient ID` = c(1, 2, 3),
  `Sex (M/F)` = c("M", "F", "M"),
  `Blood Pressure (Day 1)` = c(120, 110, 130),
  `Blood Pressure (Day 2)` = c(118, 108, 125))


messy

```

### Tidy data

```{r, echo=FALSE}
tidy <- messy %>%
  clean_names() %>%
  pivot_longer(starts_with("blood_pressure"),
               names_to = "day",
               values_to = "blood_pressure")

tidy

```

## Why use tidyverse and janitor?

-   <b>tidyverse:</b> cohesive set of packages for importing, cleaning, transforming, and visualizing data.

-   <b>janitor:</b> lightweight helpers for quick cleaning (e.g., clean_names(), tabyl(), get_dupes()).

<i>Together, they make cleaning code more readable and analysis reproducible.</i>

