# Practice

Example data used for this demonstration comes from the AMR package.

```{r}
test_data <- read.xlsx("AMR_package_data.xlsx")
```

The data is generally clean, so we will be using the package 'Messy' to intentionally make it worse so we can practice cleaning.

```{r}
#create a super messy dataframe for practicing
very_messy_test_data <- messy(test_data) %>%
  messy_colnames()


#create a messy dataframe, similar to what we might see in practice
messy_test_data <- test_data %>%
  #(cols = c("patient","ward"), messiness = 0.1) %>%
  add_whitespace(cols = "ward", messiness = 0.1) %>%
  duplicate_rows(messiness = 0.1, shuffle = TRUE) %>%
  make_missing(cols = "age", messiness = 0.1, missing = NA) 
#%>% messy_colnames() changes everytime it runs

   
```

Your challenge:
 - Clean column names
 - Check categories for inconsistencies
 - Find and remove duplicates
 - Handle missing values
 - Reshape data to long format for antibiotic results


